ll get  back to that  and the bottom band is again that simple  moving average  minus the standard deviation of price in  that time period again  multiply by x now typically time frame  is going to vary a lot more  generally it's somewhere around 20 x  i've never seen it be anything but two  but you could do you know a higher  number  especially for some stocks that are not  they're maybe volatile but they're  volatile in one direction  right so they're either going constantly  like going up or they're constantly  going down  uh you're going to find that your  bollinger bands are kind of  not working out for you so you might  want to use a higher number but  generally  20 and 2. so let's go ahead and show  some examples real quick of these  bollinger bands  let me bring up the application here  so we'll show we'll do ebay  so here's an example of ebay and their  bollinger bands  the bollinger bands are what we're  seeing right here right these three  lines  around price typically they're going to  be plotted right on there so you can  visually see the volatility there  so as you can see for the most part  price i mean ebay is a fairly volatile  one  especially coming up here but this year  you can even see how well it's  actually encapsulated the volatility  there um  so so you can see you know the  volatility like every  pretty much all the time you know prices  contained within these uh  you know the bollinger bands right so  let's look at uh another one real quick  um i'm trying to think of another good  one to do let's do uh we can do  starbucks  yeah so starbucks as you know or may not  know but starbucks has been kind of  rising in price  uh for a while now on a pretty  consistent scale  so um so you can see how much thinner  the bollinger bands are for uh  for starbucks right and so also it's  the price is pretty much every time it's  rising or falling  well at least here it was it was  contained pretty well  but especially like right here i mean  we've been 